{"topic overlaps": ["Molecular Excitation", "Collisional Rate Coefficients", "Rotational Transitions", "Astrophysical Observations", "Water in Interstellar Medium", "Deuterated Species", "Line Emission", "Thermal Balance", "Radiative Transfer", "Statistical Equilibrium"], "question": "How do the collisional rate coefficients for rotational transitions of water and deuterated water (HDO) vary with temperature, and what implications do these variations have for the interpretation of observed line intensities in high-mass star-forming regions?", "answer": "The collisional rate coefficients for rotational transitions of water (H2O) and deuterated water (HDO) vary significantly with temperature, impacting the interpretation of observed line intensities in high-mass star-forming regions. At lower temperatures (e.g., 20-100 K), the collisional rate coefficients for both H2O and HDO are generally lower, leading to sub-thermal excitation and lower line intensities. As the temperature increases (e.g., 100-2000 K), the collisional rate coefficients increase, promoting more efficient excitation and higher line intensities. This temperature dependence is crucial for understanding the physical conditions in star-forming regions, as it affects the population of rotational levels and the resulting line emission. Accurate collisional rate coefficients are essential for modeling the excitation and radiative transfer of H2O and HDO, allowing for a more precise determination of temperature, density, and abundance in these regions.", "usageJudgementGeneratorLLM": ["astro-ph/0411110", "1212.5169", "astro-ph/0506144", "0708.0345"], "papersInputtedForGeneration": [{"ArXiv": "astro-ph/0411110", "category": "astro-ph", "cosineSimilarity": 0.8850658826531264}, {"ArXiv": "1212.5169", "category": "astro-ph.GA", "cosineSimilarity": null}, {"ArXiv": "astro-ph/0506144", "category": "astro-ph", "cosineSimilarity": 0.8991647941866944}, {"ArXiv": "0708.0345", "category": "astro-ph", "cosineSimilarity