{"topic overlaps": ["Barkhausen noise", "Magnetic hysteresis", "Disorder in magnetic materials", "Avalanche distributions", "Domain wall dynamics", "Return-point memory (RPM)", "Complementary-point memory (CPM)", "Dipolar interactions", "Rate-independent hysteresis", "Critical exponents"], "question": "How do the critical exponents of avalanche distributions in magnetic materials vary with the degree of disorder, and what is the impact of dipolar interactions on these exponents?", "answer": "The critical exponents of avalanche distributions in magnetic materials, such as those observed in Barkhausen noise, vary with the degree of disorder. In systems with increasing disorder, the exponents typically show a transition from a critical to a subcritical regime. For instance, in the Random Anisotropy Ising Model (RAIM), the critical exponents for avalanche size distributions change as the disorder parameter is increased. When the disorder is below a critical threshold, the system exhibits power-law distributions indicative of critical behavior. Above this threshold, the distributions become subcritical, with a characteristic cutoff that increases with the degree of disorder. Dipolar interactions, which are long-range and antiferromagnetic in nature, can further modify these exponents. In systems where dipolar interactions are significant, the critical exponents can approach mean-field values, indicating a shift towards mean-field behavior. This is because dipolar interactions introduce additional complexity and can suppress the critical behavior observed in short-range interaction models.", "usageJudgementGeneratorLLM": ["cond-mat/0001191", "cond-mat/0012508", "cond-mat/0402406", "cond-mat/0412461", "cond-mat/0611542"], "papersInputtedForGeneration": [{"ArXiv": "cond-mat/0001191", "category": "cond-mat.mtrl-sci", "cosineSimilarity": 0.895266034831644}, {"ArXiv": "cond-mat/0611542", "category": "cond-mat.mtrl-sci", "cosineSimilarity": null}, {"ArXiv": "cond-mat/0402406", 