{"topic overlaps": ["Baryon fraction in clusters", "Cooling flows in clusters", "X-ray surface brightness profiles", "Cluster gas mass and distribution", "Temperature profiles in clusters", "Hydrostatic equilibrium in clusters", "Dark matter distribution in clusters", "Cosmological implications of cluster properties", "Intracluster medium properties", "Cluster mass determination"], "question": "How do the baryon fractions in clusters of galaxies, as determined from X-ray observations, compare to the predictions from primordial nucleosynthesis, and what are the implications for the cosmological matter density parameter Ω_m?", "answer": "X-ray observations of clusters of galaxies consistently show baryon fractions that are higher than the predictions from primordial nucleosynthesis, which suggests a mean baryon density of about 4-6% of the critical density for a flat Universe. The observed baryon fractions in clusters often exceed 10-20% of the total mass within a radius of 1 Mpc. This discrepancy, known as the 'baryon catastrophe,' implies that either the primordial nucleosynthesis predictions are incorrect, or that the baryon fraction in clusters is not representative of the universal baryon fraction. If the observed baryon fractions are correct, this would imply a lower value for the cosmological matter density parameter Ω_m, typically less than 0.3, which is in tension with a flat ΛCDM model where Ω_m is closer to 0.3. This suggests that either the clusters are not representative of the Universe as a whole, or that there are significant non-baryonic contributions to the total mass that are not accounted for in the X-ray observations.", "usageJudgementGeneratorLLM": ["astro-ph/9502092", "astro-ph/9707269", "astro-ph/9805122"], "papersInputtedForGeneration": [{"ArXiv": "astro-ph/9502092", "category": "astro-ph", "cosineSimilarity": 0.8860917783781893}, {"ArXiv": "astro-ph/9710236", "category": "astro-ph", "cosineSimilarity": 0.8810265465564043}, {"ArXiv": "astro-ph/