{"topic overlaps": ["Quark-Diquark Models", "Bethe-Salpeter Equations", "Diquark Correlations", "Hadron Structure", "Confinement", "Relativistic Effects", "Form Factors", "Rare Radiative Decays", "Effective Hamiltonians", "Three-Body Interactions"], "question": "How do the unified triquark equations, which incorporate both the Poincaré covariant quark+diquark Faddeev equations and the quasipotential quark-diquark equations, affect the calculation of form factors and the description of hadron structure, particularly in the context of rare radiative decays and the role of diquark correlations?", "answer": "The unified triquark equations, which integrate both the Poincaré covariant quark+diquark Faddeev equations and the quasipotential quark-diquark equations, provide a more comprehensive framework for describing the internal structure of hadrons. These equations account for the full range of two-body interactions, including the formation of diquark correlations, which are crucial for understanding the dynamics of baryons. In the context of rare radiative decays, such as the Ξ⁻_b → Ξ⁻γ decay, the unified equations allow for a more accurate calculation of form factors by considering the contributions from both the one-quark-exchange and the quark-quark rescattering diagrams. This approach helps in reconciling theoretical predictions with experimental data, particularly in cases where the contributions from diquark correlations are significant. The unified framework also enhances the description of hadron structure by providing a consistent picture of the roles played by quark and diquark degrees of freedom, leading to a better understanding of the underlying mechanisms of confinement and the formation of bound states.", "usageJudgementGeneratorLLM": ["2305.19024", "2208.00833", "2008.07630"], "papersInputtedForGeneration": [{"ArXiv": "2305.19024", "category": null, "cosineSimilarity": null}, {"ArXiv": "2208.00833", "category": "hep-ph", "cosineSimilarity": 0.87286863979