{"topic overlaps": ["Non-commutative space-time", "Deformation parameter", "Friedmann equations", "Cosmological models", "Quantum gravity effects", "Scale factor evolution", "Hubble parameter", "Lorentz invariance", "Star product", "Realizations of non-commutative coordinates"], "question": "How do the modifications to the Friedmann equations in non-commutative space-time, specifically in κ-deformed space-time, affect the evolution of the scale factor in radiation-, matter-, and vacuum-dominated universes, and what are the implications for the Hubble parameter and the observed Hubble tension?", "answer": "In κ-deformed space-time, the Friedmann equations are modified due to the non-commutativity of space-time coordinates. These modifications introduce a deformation parameter 'a' that affects the rate of change of the scale factor in radiation-, matter-, and vacuum-dominated universes. Specifically, the rate of change of the scale factor is altered by the non-commutativity, leading to different expansion rates depending on the sign of the deformation parameter 'a'. For positive values of 'a', the expansion and contraction rates are slower compared to the commutative case, while for negative values of 'a', these rates are faster. This modification can potentially explain the observed Hubble tension, where different measurements of the Hubble parameter yield inconsistent values. The non-commutative corrections can lead to a different expansion history of the universe, which might reconcile the discrepancies in the Hubble parameter measurements.", "usageJudgementGeneratorLLM": ["2305.09164", "hep-th/0206035", "hep-th/0702215"], "papersInputtedForGeneration": [{"ArXiv": "1303.5076", "category": "astro-ph.CO", "cosineSimilarity": 0.8661984121992229}, {"ArXiv": "2305.09164", "category": "gr-qc", "cosineSimilarity": null}, {"ArXiv": "hep-th/0206035", "category": "hep-th", "cosineSimilarity": 0.8731992887105426}, {"ArXiv": "astro-ph/0309756", "category": "astro-ph", "cosineS