{"topic overlaps": ["Form Factor Perturbation Theory", "Spectrum of Particle Excitations", "Soliton Confinement", "Mass Corrections", "Decay Widths", "Finite Volume Effects", "Resonance States", "Integrable and Non-Integrable Models", "Double Sine-Gordon Model", "Vacuum Energy Density"], "question": "How does the Form Factor Perturbation Theory (FFPT) predict the mass corrections and decay widths of particles in non-integrable models, and what are the implications for the stability and spectrum of these particles?", "answer": "Form Factor Perturbation Theory (FFPT) is a powerful tool for studying non-integrable models by perturbing integrable models. In non-integrable models, FFPT predicts mass corrections and decay widths of particles by calculating the first-order corrections to the mass and the decay widths of the particles. The mass corrections are derived from the form factors of the perturbing operator, which describe the interaction between the particles and the perturbation. The decay widths are obtained by analyzing the imaginary parts of the self-energy corrections, which arise from the non-local interactions in the perturbed theory. These predictions have significant implications for the stability and spectrum of particles in non-integrable models. Specifically, particles that are stable in the integrable limit can become unstable and decay into other particles, leading to a richer and more complex spectrum. The FFPT approach provides a systematic way to understand these transitions and the evolution of the particle spectrum as the system moves away from the integrable point.", "usageJudgementGeneratorLLM": ["hep-th/0307143", "hep-th/0607025", "hep-th/9709028", "hep-th/0604022", "hep-th/9603011"], "papersInputtedForGeneration": [{"ArXiv": "hep-th/0307143", "category": "hep-th", "cosineSimilarity": 0.8785249750799722}, {"ArXiv": "hep-th/0607025", "category": "hep-th", "cosineSimilarity": null}, {"ArXiv": "hep-th/9709028", "category": "hep-th", "cosineSimil