{"topic overlaps": ["Charge fluctuations in Coulomb systems", "Non-ideal effects in white dwarf atmospheres", "Collision-induced absorption in cool white dwarfs", "Helium-rich compositions in cool white dwarfs", "Pseudo-continuum opacity in white dwarf spectra", "Thermodynamic properties of one-component plasmas", "Atmospheric models for helium-core white dwarfs", "Occupation probability formalism in white dwarf atmospheres", "Cooling sequences of helium-core white dwarfs", "Infrared flux deficiency in cool white dwarfs"], "question": "How do non-ideal effects and collision-induced absorption impact the atmospheric models and observed spectra of cool helium-core white dwarfs, and what are the implications for their effective temperatures and atmospheric compositions?", "answer": "Non-ideal effects and collision-induced absorption play crucial roles in the atmospheric models of cool helium-core white dwarfs. Non-ideal effects, such as the perturbation of atoms and molecules by neighboring particles, can significantly alter the chemical equilibrium of the gas and its optical properties. This is particularly important for cool white dwarfs, where the high densities lead to strong interactions among particles. Collision-induced absorption (CIA) by molecular hydrogen (H₂) and helium (He) further complicates the atmospheric models by causing a strong infrared flux deficiency, which is observed in the spectra of these stars. The inclusion of these effects in atmospheric models helps to better match the observed photometric and spectroscopic data, indicating that cool helium-core white dwarfs often have helium-rich compositions. However, the models still struggle to reproduce the detailed features of the observed spectra, suggesting that additional physical processes or improved treatments of existing physics may be necessary. The effective temperatures and atmospheric compositions derived from these models are thus subject to uncertainties, but they generally indicate that