{"topic overlaps": ["High-redshift quasars", "Broad Absorption Line (BAL) quasars", "Accretion rates in AGN", "Outflow properties in AGN", "Black hole mass estimation", "Emission line properties", "Continuum shape and variability", "Dust properties in high-redshift quasars", "Reverberation mapping", "Quasar host galaxy properties"], "question": "How do the properties of outflows and accretion rates in high-redshift quasars (z > 4) compare to those in lower-redshift quasars, and what implications do these differences have for the evolution of quasars and their host galaxies?", "answer": "High-redshift quasars (z > 4) exhibit several distinctive properties in their outflows and accretion rates compared to lower-redshift quasars. High-redshift quasars often show a higher fraction of Broad Absorption Line (BAL) quasars, with deeper and more prominent absorption features, indicating larger amounts of dense gas outflowing at high velocities. These outflows are likely associated with higher accretion rates, which are expected in the early stages of quasar activity. The higher accretion rates in high-redshift quasars can lead to more energetic outflows, which can have significant feedback effects on the host galaxies, potentially regulating star formation and influencing the co-evolution of the black hole and the galaxy. In contrast, lower-redshift quasars typically have less prominent outflows and lower accretion rates, suggesting a more mature phase of quasar activity. The differences in outflow properties and accretion rates between high- and low-redshift quasars provide insights into the evolutionary stages of quasars and their host galaxies, supporting models of rapid black hole growth and strong feedback mechanisms in the early universe.", "usageJudgementGeneratorLLM": ["2305.12492", "astro-ph/0312402", "astro-ph/0402453", "2309.13418", "astro-ph/0105231"], "papersInputtedForGeneration": [{"ArXiv": "2305.12492", "category": "astro-ph.GA", "cosineSimilarity": null}, {"