{"topic overlaps": ["Atmospheric Circulation Models", "Equatorial Superrotation", "Day-Night Temperature Contrast", "Thermal Structure and Composition", "Rossby Waves", "Planetary Rotation and Synchronization", "Observational Constraints", "Magnetic Field Interactions", "Radiative Transfer and Heating", "Atmospheric Variability"], "question": "How do the interactions between Rossby waves and the mean flow contribute to the formation of equatorial superrotation in the atmospheres of tidally locked exoplanets, and what are the implications for the observed day-night temperature contrasts?", "answer": "The formation of equatorial superrotation in the atmospheres of tidally locked exoplanets is primarily driven by the interaction between Rossby waves and the mean flow. Rossby waves, which are naturally excited by the day-night thermal forcing, develop phase tilts that pump eastward momentum from high latitudes to the equator. This process results in a fast eastward jet stream at the equator, which can cause the hottest regions to be displaced eastward from the substellar point. The equatorial superrotation is a key feature in the atmospheric circulation of these planets, contributing to the observed day-night temperature contrasts. The strength and extent of this superrotation depend on factors such as the amplitude of the thermal forcing, the strength of friction, and the radiative time constants. Observational constraints, such as infrared light curves, can provide evidence for the presence of equatorial superrotation and help refine our understanding of the atmospheric dynamics of tidally locked exoplanets.", "usageJudgementGeneratorLLM": ["astro-ph/0502476", "astro-ph/0209227", "1401.3673", "1202.0026", "1103.3101"], "papersInputtedForGeneration": [{"ArXiv": "astro-ph/0502476", "category": "astro-ph", "cosineSimilarity": 0.8716131720235457}, {"ArXiv": "astro-ph/0209227", "category": "astro-ph", "cosineSimilarity": 0.8812791024941199}, {"ArXiv": "1401.3673", "categor