{"topic overlaps": ["Entanglement-assisted quantum error correction", "Quantum low-density parity-check (LDPC) codes", "Classical-to-quantum code conversion", "Entanglement consumption in quantum codes", "Performance of quantum codes over depolarizing channels", "Sum-product algorithm for decoding", "Girth of LDPC codes", "Catalytic quantum error correction", "Entanglement-assisted capacity", "Strong converse theorems"], "question": "How does the use of pre-shared entanglement in entanglement-assisted quantum LDPC codes affect the performance and efficiency of quantum error correction, and what are the implications for constructing large-scale quantum codes?", "answer": "The use of pre-shared entanglement in entanglement-assisted quantum LDPC (EAQ-LDPC) codes significantly enhances the performance and efficiency of quantum error correction. By leveraging pre-shared entanglement, the dual-containing constraint of classical codes is relaxed, allowing for the construction of quantum codes from a broader range of classical LDPC codes. This relaxation enables the use of classical LDPC codes with better distance properties and fewer short cycles, which are detrimental to the performance of iterative decoding algorithms like the sum-product algorithm. As a result, EAQ-LDPC codes can achieve better error correction performance, especially in the presence of depolarizing channels. Additionally, the ability to use classical codes with larger minimum distances and fewer 4-cycles leads to improved decoding performance and reduced entanglement consumption, making it feasible to construct large-scale, efficient quantum codes. This approach not only simplifies the construction of quantum codes but also paves the way for practical implementations in quantum communication and potentially in quantum computing.", "usageJudgementGeneratorLLM": ["0912.3805", "0803.0100", "quant-ph/0608027", "1310.7028", "0906.5532"], "papersInputtedForGeneration": [{"ArXiv": "1310.7028", "category": "qu