{"topic overlaps": ["Localization of fermions on topological defects", "Hierarchical mass patterns of fermions", "Topological defects with winding numbers", "Chiral fermionic zero modes", "Fermion mass generation via wave function overlaps", "Mixing angles and fermion generations", "Gauge and global symmetries in extra dimensions", "Abrikosov-Nielsen-Olesen vortices", "Scalar field interactions with fermions", "Yukawa couplings and fermion masses"], "question": "How do the profiles of fermionic wave functions in extra dimensions contribute to the hierarchical mass pattern and mixing angles observed in the Standard Model?", "answer": "The hierarchical mass pattern and mixing angles observed in the Standard Model can be explained by the different profiles of fermionic wave functions in extra dimensions. In models where fermions are localized on topological defects, such as vortices with specific winding numbers, the fermionic zero modes have distinct radial and angular wave functions. These wave functions overlap differently with the Higgs field, leading to varying effective Yukawa couplings. The overlap integrals, which determine the masses, are sensitive to the spatial distribution of the wave functions. For instance, in the case of a gauged vortex, the first mode is more localized in the core, while the second and third modes have broader distributions, leading to a hierarchical mass pattern. Mixing angles arise from the off-diagonal terms in the mass matrix, which are suppressed by the small overlaps of the wave functions, especially when there is a small but explicit violation of the global symmetry. This mechanism ensures that the mass hierarchy and mixing angles are consistent with the observed values in the Standard Model.", "usageJudgementGeneratorLLM": ["hep-th/0310231", "hep-th/9712153", "hep-ph/0110045", "hep-ph/0201162", "hep-ph/0011095"], "papersInputtedForGeneration": [{"ArXiv": "hep-th/0310231", "category": "hep-th", "cosineSimilarity": null}, {"ArXiv"