{"topic overlaps": ["Model Merging", "Parameter Efficiency", "Task-Specific Capabilities", "Large Language Models", "Supervised Fine-Tuning", "Gradient Noise Scale", "Data Parallelism", "Batch Size Optimization", "Multi-Objective Optimization", "Pareto Optimal Solutions"], "question": "How does the gradient noise scale influence the optimal batch size for training large language models, and what are the implications for model merging and parameter efficiency?", "answer": "The gradient noise scale is a key metric that predicts the largest effective batch size for training large language models. When the batch size is much smaller than the noise scale, increasing the batch size leads to almost linear speedups in training. However, when the batch size exceeds the noise scale, further increases in batch size provide minimal benefits. This transition point is crucial for optimizing data parallelism and total resource use. In the context of model merging, the noise scale can help in determining the optimal batch size for merging multiple task-specific models, ensuring that the merged model retains the necessary task-specific capabilities without wasting computational resources. Parameter efficiency is also enhanced by using the noise scale to guide the merging process, as it helps in identifying the most effective parameters to retain, thereby reducing redundancy and improving the overall performance of the merged model.", "usageJudgementGeneratorLLM": ["1812.06162", "2311.03099", "2406.07529"], "papersInputtedForGeneration": [{"ArXiv": "1709.00029", "category": "cs.CV", "cosineSimilarity": 0.8205819689939909}, {"ArXiv": "2406.07529", "category": "cs.LG", "cosineSimilarity": null}, {"ArXiv": "2310.11564", "category": "cs.CL", "cosineSimilarity": 0.8921961284302169}, {"ArXiv": "2311.03099", "category": "cs.CL", "cosineSimilarity": 0.8901904199315093}, {"ArXiv": "1812.06162", "category": "cs.LG", "cosineSimilarity": 0.8648684317533468}], "anchorPaper": "2406.07529", "adapti